  number = {7},
  month = {August},
  abstract = {Regionalization is a classification procedure applied to spatial objects
	with an areal representation, which groups them into homogeneous
	contiguous regions. This paper presents an efficient method for regionalization.
	The first step creates a connectivity graph that captures the neighbourhood
	relationship between the spatial objects. The cost of each edge in
	the graph is inversely proportional to the similarity between the
	regions it joins. We summarize the neighbourhood structure by a minimum
	spanning tree (MST), which is a connected tree with no circuits.
	We partition the MST by successive removal of edges that link dissimilar
	regions. The result is the division of the spatial objects into connected
	regions that have maximum internal homogeneity. Since the MST partitioning
