	living reduces social capital and thus social interaction, and this
	argument has been used to buttress criticisms of urban sprawl. If
	low densities in fact reduce social interaction, then an externality
	arises, validating Putnam?s critique. In choosing their own lot sizes,
	consumers would fail to consider the loss of interaction benefits
