	an alternative "one-sided" tipping model in which neighborhoods with
	a minority share below a critical threshold are potentially stable,
	but those that exceed the threshold rapidly shift to 100% minority
	composition. In this paper we examine the racial dynamics of Census
	tracts in major metropolitan areas over the period from 1970 to 2000,
	focusing on the question of whether tipping is "two-sided" or "one-sided".
