  institution = {National Bureau of Economic Research},
  year = {2010},
  type = {Working Paper},
  number = {No. 16615},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  abstract = {This paper proposes a simple theory of a system of cities that decomposes
	the determinants of the city size distribution into three main components:
	efficiency, amenities, and frictions. Higher efficiency and better
	amenities lead to larger cities, but also to greater frictions through
	congestion and other negative effects of agglomeration. Using data
	on MSAs in the United States, we parametrize the model and empirically
	estimate efficiency, amenities and frictions. Counterfactual exercises
	show that all three characteristics are important in that eliminating
	any of them leads to large population reallocations, though the welfare
	effects from these reallocations are small. Overall, we find that
	the gains from worker mobility across cities are modest. When allowing
	for externalities, we find an important city selection effect: eliminating
	differences in any of the city characteristics causes many cities
	to exit. We apply the same methodology to Chinese cities and find
	welfare effects that are many times larger than in the U.S.},
