  pages = {539--575},
  abstract = {This paper critically examines the interdisciplinary research of neighborhood
	effects. Neighborhood effects are community influences on individual
	social or economic outcomes. Examples include labor force activity,
	child outcomes, criminal behavior, and other socioeconomic phenomena.
	The existing theoretical and empirical literature is reviewed. Conceptual
	definitions from sociology are linked and contrasted with economic
	models. Early studies are criticized for failing to account for a
	number of endogeneity concerns. Recent empirical studies are also
	explored. The results of these papers demonstrate that although neighborhood
