  pages = {332--342},
  number = {4},
  abstract = {Very little is known about what impact recent large upward and downward
	swings in single-family home values have had on local government
	budgets. Using a unique 15-year panel of Florida cities that includes
	both detailed revenue and house price data, we investigate the pathways
	whereby a change in house price may affect city revenue per capita
	and test for symmetric effects during housing booms and busts. For
	the median-sized city, we find that while increases in house price
	raise revenues, decreases in price have no effect on revenues. In
	addition, the former impact is small in magnitude. While the strongest
	pathway is through assessed values, our results illustrate that a
