	We refer to both the Cambridge capital controversies and the aggregation
	conditions. The most salient results are summarized, and the problems
	that economists should be aware of from incorrect aggregation are
	discussed. The most important conclusion is that the conditions under
	which a well-behaved aggregate production function can be derived
