  type = {Staff Report},
  number = {No. 594},
  address = {New York},
  abstract = {Fixed-rate mortgages (FRMs) dominate the U.S. mortgage market, with
	important consequences for household risk management, monetary policy,
	and systemic risk. In this paper, we show that securitization is
	a key driver of FRM supply. Our analysis compares the agency and
	nonagency mortgage-backed-securities (MBS) markets, exploiting the
	freeze in nonagency MBS liquidity in the third quarter of 2007. Using
	exogenous variation in access to the agency MBS market, we find that
	when both market segments are liquid they perform similarly in terms
	of supporting FRM supply. However, after the nonagency market freezes,
	the share of FRMs is sharply higher among mortgages eligible to be
	securitized through the still-liquid agency MBS market. Our interpretation
