	with risk in some markets but negatively in others. This paper rationalizes
	these cross-market differences in the risk-return relationship for
	housing, and in so doing, explains the puzzling negative relationship.
	The paper shows that when the current house provides a hedge against
	the risk associated with the future housing consumption, households
