	to make significant improvements. While Angrist and Pischke extol
	the successes of empirical work that estimates "treatment effects"
	based on actual or quasi-experiments, they are much less sanguine
	about structural analysis and hold industrial organization up as
	an example where "progress is less dramatic." Indeed, reading their
	article one comes away with the impression that there is only a single
	way to conduct credible empirical analysis. This seems to us a very
	narrow and dogmatic approach to empirical work; credible analysis
	can come in many guises, both structural and nonstructural, and for
	some questions structural analysis offers important advantages. In
	this comment, we address the criticism of structural analysis and
