  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  month = {October},
  abstract = {We show that the hedging benefit of owning a home reduces the variability
	of housing consumption after a move. When a current home owner's
	house price covaries positively with housing costs in a future city,
	changes in the future cost of housing are offset by commensurate
	changes in wealth before the move. Using Census micro-data, we find
	that the cross-sectional variation in house values subsequent to
	a move is lower for home owners who moved between more highly covarying
	cities. Our preferred estimates imply that an increase in covariance
