	we develop this integrationist approach to finance in ways that retain
	political economies of states, markets, and social power in our interpretations
	of geographically uneven development. In this article, we discuss
	the plural nature of emergent work on financialization and develop
	three analytical themes to shape our discussion of financialization.
	Next, we elaborate our analytical approach by warning against functional,
	political, and spatial disconnections traced in the literature on
	the geographies of money. We then explore how financialization is
	broadening and deepening the array of agents, relations, and sites
	that require consideration in economic geography and is generating
