  number = {6\&7},
  abstract = {Contemporary research into the evolution of regional economies is
	characterized by a dualism of �new� economic geographies based upon
	dominant ways of knowing in both economics and geography. This dualism
	of theoretical and methodological perspectives encompasses both qualitative
	versus quantitative ways of knowing, and cultural versus economic
	explanations of regional growth and change. In this paper I explore
	an alternative way of thinking about regions that is grounded in
	the theoretical suppositions of classical/Marxian political economy,
