	begin with a critical review of Michael Klein and Jay C. Shambaugh�s
	(2010) book Exchange Rate Regimes in the Modern Era, and then proceed
	to provide an alternative overview of what the economics profession
	knows and needs to know about exchange rate regimes. While a fixed
	exchange rate with capital mobility is a well-defined monetary regime,
	floating is not; thus, it is unclear whether it is theoretically
	sensible to compare countries across exchange rate regimes. This
	comparison is quite difficult to make empirically. It is often hard
	to figure out what the exchange rate regime of a country is in practice,
	since there are multiple conflicting regime classifications. More
	importantly, similar countries choose radically different exchange
	rate regimes without substantive consequences for macroeconomic outcomes
