  number = {3},
  abstract = {Morris A. Copeland's work and career as an institutional economist
	has been the subject of only one significant paper (Millar 1980)
	and is not well known today, even among institutionalists. The standard
	histories of institutional economics have tended to focus on a few
	leading figures, notably Thorstein Veblen, Wesley C. Mitchell, John
	R. Commons, and Clarence Ayres, and have largely ignored the many
	others (some of whom had outstanding careers) associated with the
	movement. This narrow focus has given a misleading impression both
	of the institutionalist movement itself and also of the nature of
	the institutionalist contribution to economics. The examination of
	Copeland's career, treated as a case study in the history of institutional
	economics, can help correct this problem. Copeland is a perfect candidate
