  number = {5},
  abstract = {An extensive empirical literature exists, showing that variations
	in region-specific amenities can account for persistent differences
	in real wages across regions. However, this literature has considered
	only amenities in the same location as the household. This paper
	argues that environmental amenities at some distance from but accessible
	to urban areas may lead to negative compensating wage differentials.We
	use a general equilibrium framework and data from the 1995 Current
	Population Survey to calculate implicit amenity prices based on measures
	of distance to environmental amenities. Our results suggest that
	amenities outside the metropolitan area do generate compensating
	wage differentials, as workers are willing to accept lower wages
	to live in accessible proximity to �nice� places. This implies that
