  number = {12},
  abstract = {This paper seeks to understand how recent social, economic and institutional
	developments have affected the land use planning tools and instruments
	that German planners have at their disposal. Although traditional
	planning practice was focused on the equitable distribution of services
	and infrastructure and managing growth within a highly structured
	plan approval process, planning tools at both the local and regional
	level have become increasingly concerned with enhancing local or
	regional competitiveness, primarily through the inclusion of a greater
	number of actors in formulating land use decisions. I argue, however,
	that despite these changes, the overall institutional framework,
	which revolves around legal and procedural concerns such as the plan
