  number = {5},
  abstract = {Green economics is arising from a study of the economy that takes
	a philosophical position characterised by a deep respect for nature.
	It is primarily a system of ideas and principles, rather than a rationally
	argued intellectual position. Its ideas are powerful and influential
	on developments in policy and politics, but it is presently less
	well grounded in the academy. Green economists do not dwell overmuch
	on introspection and their method is implicit rather than explicit,
	relying on a grounded, embedded and phenomenological approach and
	rejecting the scientism and spurious objectivity of neoclassical
	economics. In this paper I outline four key issues central to a green
