  number = {1},
  month = {January},
  abstract = {Geography plays a prominent role in many problems in development economics--directly
	in analyses of the spatial distribution of important variables like
	poverty and productivity, and indirectly through the role of local
	spillovers in economic growth. Empirical work on these topics is
	complicated by the fact that the behavioral consequences of such
	spillovers cannot be distinguished from those of unobservable local
	attributes using only the observed location decisions of individuals
	or firms. This problem can be solved with an instrumental variables
