  number = {2--3},
  abstract = {One reason for the United States� economic success is the willingness
	of its residents to follow jobs. Households� decisions to move depend
	not only on job prospects but also on the relative cost of housing.
	I investigate how the housing market affects the flow of workers
	across cities. This occurs through at least two channels: the relative
	mobility of homeowners versus renters, and the relative cost of housing
	across markets. I use homeownership rates to measure the former,
	and use an index that measures house prices across metropolitan statistical
	areas (MSAs) and the price elasticity of housing supply to capture
